#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3. First principles"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "These are the ideas the patterns rest on. Each is stated as a claim, with the source it comes from."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.1 Event time is not processing time, and completeness is a guess"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Akidau et al. separate *event time* from *processing time* and argue that a system processing unbounded, out-of-order data \"will never know if or when we have seen all of our data, only that new data will arrive, old data may be retracted\" [1]. The Dataflow Model makes the trade-off explicit with four questions: *what* is computed, *where* in event time (windows), *when* in processing time results are emitted (triggers, driven by watermarks), and *how* refinements relate (discarding, accumulating, or accumulating-and-retracting panes) [1]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "*Consequences for backfills.* (a) Partition by event time, not load time, or a re-run of \"yesterday\" means nothing stable. (b) A *watermark* is a heuristic statement that input for a time range is probably complete; backfills exist largely because that heuristic is sometimes wrong. (c) When a backfill changes a previously emitted result, downstream needs either a replacement of the whole result (discarding/overwrite) or an explicit retraction. Silent in-place mutation is neither."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.2 The log is the source of truth; tables are projections"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Kreps: a log of all changes \"from the beginning\" is a complete representation of a dataset; tables and indexes are projections of it, and can be rebuilt by replaying it [2]. In \"Questioning the Lambda Architecture\" he proposes *Kappa* reprocessing: when logic changes, start a second instance of the job from the beginning of the retained log, write to a **new** output table, switch readers when it catches up, then delete the old job and table [3]. This removes the Lambda requirement to maintain the same logic twice (batch and speed layers) [3]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Helland makes the general case: immutable data is what lets distributed systems coordinate, and updatable stores are layered on immutable files (LSM trees, copy-on-write) [5]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "*Consequence.* Keep raw inputs immutable and retained long enough to replay. If the raw input is gone, a logic-change backfill (S2) is impossible, and you are restating from a derived table, which carries forward its errors."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.3 Functional data engineering: pure tasks over immutable partitions"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Beauchemin (2018) applies functional programming to batch pipelines [6]: tasks are *pure* (same inputs give the same output, no side effects beyond writing their own target), *idempotent*, and write *immutable partitions* that are replaced whole, never mutated. Raw data goes in a *persistent staging area* kept indefinitely so any partition can be recomputed; slowly changing dimensions are handled by snapshotting dimensions per partition rather than mutating rows. With these rules, a backfill is just running the same task for older partitions."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.4 Idempotency and exactly-once *effects*"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "8"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Delivery across a network is at-least-once in practice; \"exactly once\" is achieved as an *effect* by making the write idempotent [7]{1}. Helland defines idempotence as an operation whose repetition has the same effect as doing it once, and argues it is a requirement in any system that retries messages [7]. Practical ways to get it:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Deterministic keys.** Derive the row key from the content or the domain (natural key, or a hash of stable fields), never from load time or a sequence assigned during the load. A retried load then produces the same keys and a MERGE or overwrite makes it a no-op."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Slice replacement.** Replace the whole slice atomically (section 4.1)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Commit tokens.** Record the (job id, slice id or version) inside the same atomic commit as the data, and skip a commit whose token is already present. Delta Lake's `txnAppId`/`txnVersion` options do exactly this: a write with a version at or below the last recorded one for that app id is ignored [16]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.5 Determinism of transformations"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "A backfill is only comparable to the original run if the transformation is deterministic: same inputs and same logic version produce byte-identical (or at least row-identical) outputs. Sources of non-determinism to remove: wall-clock reads (`now()`), unseeded randomness, iteration over unordered containers, `LIMIT` without `ORDER BY`, floating-point aggregation order, \"latest\" lookups resolved at run time rather than as of the slice's event time, and dependence on the run date rather than the slice date. Determinism is what makes parallel-run diffs (section 4.8) meaningful: any diff is then caused by logic or input, not noise. (First principles; no single source.)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.6 Bitemporal modelling"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Snodgrass distinguishes *valid time* (when a fact holds in reality) from *transaction time* (when the database knew it) and shows how to model, query and correct both in SQL [10]. SQL:2011 standardised application-time period tables (valid time), system-versioned tables (transaction time) and bitemporal tables combining both [11]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "*Why backfills need it.* A backfill that corrects history is a *transaction time* event about *valid time* facts. Without both axes you cannot answer \"what did we report for March, as of the April board meeting?\", and a restatement erases the evidence of what was previously published. Lakehouse table formats give you transaction time for free at table granularity (time travel to a snapshot) but not per row; per-row `recorded_at` / `superseded_at` columns are needed when consumers must see corrections explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.7 Slowly changing dimensions"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Kimball's SCD types [12]: type 1 overwrites the attribute (history lost); type 2 adds a row with effective dates (history kept); type 3 adds a \"previous value\" column; types 0, 4, 5, 6 and 7 are variants and hybrids (DT #152). For backfills the rule is: **a type 2 dimension must be backfilled in event-time order**, because each new version closes the previous one. Loading out of order either produces overlapping validity ranges or requires a rebuild of the key's whole history. A safe alternative is to rebuild the full type 2 history for affected keys from the immutable change log in one pass (Beauchemin's \"dimension snapshots\" [6] give the same result without ordering constraints)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "3.8 Recompute versus incremental maintenance"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "The view-maintenance literature frames the choice: a derived table can be fully recomputed, or maintained incrementally from deltas of its inputs [13]. Incremental maintenance is cheaper per change but is only correct for certain classes of view (for example, aggregates that are self-maintainable under inserts, but not always under deletes or updates), and it accumulates any past error. Full recompute is always correct relative to its inputs and logic but costs proportional to history size."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "22"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "*Rule.* Normal operation may be incremental; **a logic change (S2) requires recompute** of every slice the change affects, because incremental maintenance applies the new logic only to new deltas. Many orchestration tools (dbt `--full-refresh`, microbatch with an explicit range) are interfaces to this choice [21]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4. Pattern catalogue: analytical platform"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Each pattern uses the same fields: **Problem**, **Forces**, **Solution**, **Consequences**, **Verification**, **Sources**, and **Maps to** (a) open-source stack, (b) Databricks."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.1 Slice replacement (partition overwrite)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Re-running a time slice of a derived table (S2, S3) must not double-count or leave stale rows."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Appends are cheap but not idempotent. Row-level deletes before insert are two operations and can half-fail. Readers must never see the slice empty or doubled."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Treat the slice (partition or predicate range) as the unit of work. Compute the full slice, then atomically replace exactly that slice in one commit: `INSERT OVERWRITE` with dynamic partition overwrite, or an overwrite with an explicit predicate (`replaceWhere`). The predicate must be the same one the computation used to select inputs."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Re-runs are idempotent and order-independent across slices. The slice must be computable from inputs alone (pure task, 3.3). It does not suit tables without a natural slice. Danger: in Spark's default **static** overwrite mode, an `INSERT OVERWRITE` without a `PARTITION` clause replaces **all** partitions; Iceberg's docs show it dropping every row in the table while writing one day [17]. Use dynamic mode or an explicit predicate, always. Iceberg recommends `MERGE INTO` over dynamic overwrite because overwritten scope can change if partitioning evolves [17]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Row count and checksum of the slice before and after; assert that rows outside the predicate are unchanged (compare snapshot diffs: the commit must touch only files of that slice); run the backfill twice and assert identical outputs."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "16"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [6]{1}[17]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Spark + Iceberg with `spark.sql.sources.partitionOverwriteMode=dynamic`, or Delta `replaceWhere`; dbt `insert_overwrite` strategy. (b) Delta `replaceWhere` / `INSERT INTO ... REPLACE WHERE`, dynamic partition overwrite; Delta `txnAppId`/`txnVersion` for commit-level idempotency."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.2 Keyed upsert with recency guard"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Loading or correcting entities identified by a key (learners, institutions, sheets), where the backfill may carry older versions than what is already there."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** MERGE is idempotent only if keys are deterministic. A backfill running alongside live ingestion can overwrite a fresher row with a stale one. Duplicate keys in the source make MERGE fail or be non-deterministic."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** `MERGE` on the **natural key**. Add a **recency guard** to the update clause: update only if the incoming version is newer by a monotonic version field (source LSN, source commit sequence, record version), e.g. `WHEN MATCHED AND s.src_version > t.src_version THEN UPDATE`. Deduplicate the source per key first (keep the highest version) so at most one source row matches each target row. For deletes, carry tombstones with a version rather than inferring deletion from absence."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Safe to run concurrently with live ingestion and safe to repeat. Requires a trustworthy version field; wall-clock `updated_at` is a weak substitute (see 4.3). MERGE rewrites files containing matched rows, so large backfills via MERGE can be slower than slice replacement."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** For a sample of keys, assert the target holds the max-version row from all sources. Re-run and assert zero changed rows. Count of keys in target = count of distinct keys in sources minus tombstoned."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "17"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [7]{1}; Iceberg notes MERGE requires at most one source row per target row [17]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Iceberg/Delta `MERGE INTO`; dbt incremental `merge` with `unique_key`; Postgres `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (key) DO UPDATE ... WHERE excluded.v > t.v`. (b) Delta `MERGE INTO`; Lakeflow declarative pipelines' `AUTO CDC` / `APPLY CHANGES` with a sequencing column does the same guarded upsert **[mapping not re-checked against current docs]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.3 High-water-mark incremental extract (with overlap)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Pull only new or changed rows from a source table."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Cheap and simple; needs only an `updated_at` or monotonically increasing id. But commit order is not timestamp order."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Record the high-water mark (HWM) after each successful load. Next run reads `updated_at >= HWM - overlap`, deduplicates on key and version, and upserts (4.2). Advance the HWM only after the write commits, and only to `min(max_seen, now - safety_lag)`. Prefer a commit-ordered cursor where the source has one (log sequence number, Postgres `xmin`-based cursors, a sequence assigned at commit by an outbox)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Without overlap, rows are lost: in Postgres, `now()` returns the *start time of the transaction* [18], so a long transaction that starts at 10:00 and commits at 10:05 writes `updated_at = 10:00`; if the extractor ran at 10:02 and advanced its HWM to 10:02, the row is never seen. Clock skew across application servers has the same effect [9]. Hard deletes are invisible to HWM extraction. Overlap creates duplicates, so the sink must be idempotent (4.2)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Periodic full-key reconciliation between source and sink (4.8): count and key-set comparison per day. Alert if the HWM stops moving or moves backward."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "18"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [9]{1}; first principles."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Airflow/Dagster task with a stored cursor; dbt incremental models with `is_incremental()` filter on `>=` and a lookback. (b) Same in Lakeflow Jobs; prefer Lakeflow Connect CDC for databases where available."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.4 Snapshot and log stitching (onboarding a live source)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Onboard a live OLTP source with years of history (S4): you need a full copy plus every change after it, with no gap and no lost update at the seam, and without locking the source."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** A consistent full snapshot of a big table takes hours. Locking tables for that time is not acceptable. Starting CDC \"after\" the snapshot leaves a gap; starting it \"before\" creates overlaps that must be resolved per key. Snapshot must be resumable."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "19"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** DBLog (Netflix) [14]: keep the change-log stream running continuously. Snapshot the table in **chunks** ordered by primary key. For each chunk, write a *low watermark* event into the log (via a small watermark table in the source), select the chunk, write a *high watermark*. When the log reader reaches the high watermark, it drops from the chunk any row whose key appeared in log events between low and high, because the log event is newer, and emits the rest of the chunk before continuing. No locks, the log never stalls, and chunks can be paused and resumed. Debezium's incremental snapshots implement the same algorithm, triggered by inserting an `execute-snapshot` signal (optionally with an `additional-conditions` filter) into a signalling table; chunk size defaults to 1,024 rows and progress is stored in connector offsets so the snapshot resumes after a crash [15]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Requires primary keys. Consumers see a mix of `read` and `update` events for the same key in non-obvious order, so downstream must be an idempotent keyed upsert (4.2) [15]. Adds a watermark/signal table to the source schema. Can also re-snapshot a subset (a table, a key range) to repair a gap, which makes it a gap-fill tool (S3) as well as an onboarding tool."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** After snapshot completion, reconcile row count and a key hash per table (or per key range) between source (read from a replica at a known LSN) and sink (4.8). Check that the replication slot lag returned to baseline."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "15"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [14]{1}[19]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Debezium on Kafka/Kafka Connect or Debezium Server → Iceberg/Delta via MERGE; Postgres logical replication slots. (b) Lakeflow Connect for supported databases (it manages the initial snapshot and CDC); otherwise Debezium into a landing zone and declarative-pipeline CDC upsert **[Lakeflow Connect internals not verified]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.5 Transactional outbox"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** An operational service must publish its changes (and, for onboarding, its history) to the platform reliably."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Writing to the database and to a broker are two non-atomic operations (\"dual write\"); either can fail after the other succeeds. Distributed transactions are rarely available or desirable."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Write the business change and an event row to an `outbox` table **in the same database transaction**. A relay (poller or log-based CDC on the outbox table) publishes outbox rows in commit order [20]. For history, the service (or a one-off job) writes synthetic \"snapshot\" events for existing entities into the same outbox, keyed and versioned like live events, so history and live use one path."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** At-least-once: the relay can publish a row twice after a crash, so consumers must be idempotent [20]. Ordering is preserved per aggregate. The outbox needs pruning. Developers can forget to write the event."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Consumers record the last processed event id per aggregate; reconcile counts of outbox rows versus consumed events per day."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "14"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [20]{1} (DBLog's motivation section rejects dual writes for the same reason)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Postgres outbox table + Debezium outbox event router. (b) Lakebase table as outbox + CDC/sync to Delta **[Lakebase-to-Delta sync mechanics not re-verified here]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.6 Shadow recompute and swap (blue/green tables)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Recompute a whole table with new logic (S2) without consumers seeing a half-updated mix of old and new results, and with a way back."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** In-place rewrites expose partial states and destroy the old answer. Recompute takes time; live data keeps arriving during it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Kreps' Kappa reprocessing generalised [3]: run the new logic from the beginning of retained input into a **new** table (or a branch, 4.7), let it catch up with live input, compare it with the old output (4.8), then switch consumers by an atomic pointer change (a view redefinition, a catalog alias, a branch fast-forward, or a table rename). Keep the old table for a defined fallback window, then drop it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Double storage and compute during the overlap. Readers see either all-old or all-new. Requires that raw inputs are retained (3.2). Consumers that cached results need a restatement notice (4.11)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Parallel-run diff (4.8): every difference between old and new must be explained by the logic change. A diff of zero on the slices the change should not affect is a strong regression test."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "2"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [3]{1}[4]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) New Iceberg table or branch, swap via view or catalog rename; dbt with a versioned model (`v2`) and a view that points at the active version. (b) Delta table per version, consumers read through a Unity Catalog view; swap by `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.7 Write-audit-publish (WAP) on a branch"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** A backfill writes a lot of data; any defect becomes visible to consumers the moment it commits."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Quality checks need the data written somewhere first. Copying data to staging and back doubles I/O. Publication should be atomic."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** **Write** to an isolated version of the table (a branch, a staging table, or a hidden snapshot); **audit** it with data-quality checks and reconciliation; **publish** by an atomic metadata operation. The pattern was popularised by Netflix (DataWorks Summit 2017, \"Whoops, the numbers are wrong! Scaling data quality @ Netflix\") **[unverified]**. In Iceberg: set `write.wap.enabled=true`, create an audit branch, set `spark.wap.branch`, write, validate, then `CALL system.fast_forward('db.t', 'main', 'audit-branch')` [21a]. Nessie extends branches to **multi-table** commits, so a backfill that touches a dimension and its fact tables publishes atomically across all of them [23]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Publication is a pointer move, so rollback is too. Branches have retention; the audit branch must not expire before publish. Single-table branches do not give cross-table atomicity; consumers may see the new dimension with the old facts unless you publish together or use a multi-table catalog."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** The audit step is the verification: schema checks, null/uniqueness constraints, reconciliation totals, distribution drift against the previous version. Deequ-style declarative constraints run as aggregation queries are one systematic form [24]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "23"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [21a]{1}[24]; Netflix talk **[unverified]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Iceberg branches + `fast_forward`; Nessie for multi-table; lakeFS is an alternative for file-level branching. (b) Delta has no table branches: use a **shallow clone** as the write target, audit it, then publish with `replaceWhere`/MERGE or a view swap. Note shallow clones reference source files and break if the source is `VACUUM`ed [25]. Delta time travel gives rollback after publish."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.8 Reconciliation and parallel-run diff"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Proving a backfill is complete and correct."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Full row-by-row comparison across systems is expensive. Legacy and new systems encode things differently (types, time zones, codes). Job success says nothing about data."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Three tiers, cheapest first:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Counts** per slice (date, school, source file) in source and target."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Control totals**: sums of additive measures, min/max of keys and timestamps, count of distinct keys, per slice."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Hashes**: per-row hash of the canonicalised business columns, then an order-independent aggregate (e.g. sum or XOR of row hashes, or a Merkle tree over key ranges) per slice; drill into mismatching slices only."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "27"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "For logic changes, run old and new logic in parallel on the same inputs and **diff** outputs per key; classify each difference as expected (caused by the change) or a defect. GitHub's Scientist library applies the same idea to code paths in production; Stripe used it while migrating hundreds of millions of subscription records [26]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Canonicalisation must be agreed (trim, case, time zone, decimal scale), or every row mismatches. Hash tiers miss nothing they cover but say nothing about columns excluded from the hash. Reconciliation must read the source at a known consistent point (a replica at an LSN, a frozen extract), or it races live writes."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** The reconciliation report is itself an artefact: stored with the backfill run id, logic version and the source position it was computed against."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "26"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [24]{1}[27]; Fowler/Thoughtworks on parallel running [28]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) SQL queries or a diffing tool over both sides; Great Expectations / Deequ / dbt tests for constraints. (b) Same via Databricks SQL; Lakehouse Federation lets one query read the legacy database and Delta side by side **[federation coverage of a given legacy DB not verified]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.9 Throttled, chunked extraction (protect the source)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Reading years of history from a production database or a rate-limited API without hurting live users."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Big scans evict cache, saturate I/O, and on MVCC databases a long-running read transaction holds back vacuum and can bloat tables. APIs enforce rate limits. A failed eight-hour extract that restarts from zero is wasteful."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Read from a **replica**, a **snapshot/backup restore**, or a **branch** (5.2) instead of the primary. Split the work into **chunks** by primary-key range (not `OFFSET`), each a short transaction; persist the last completed chunk so the job resumes. **Throttle** on feedback signals: replication lag, source CPU/latency, API `429`/`Retry-After`, and back off exponentially. Make throttle parameters adjustable while running. gh-ost is the reference design: pausable, throttles on replica lag and custom checks, with chunk size and max lag changeable at runtime [29]. Stripe avoided production queries entirely by backfilling from offline Hadoop snapshots [27]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Slower wall-clock time, by design. Chunked reads are not a single consistent snapshot; combine with log stitching (4.4) or read from a frozen copy when you need consistency."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Monitor source latency and replica lag during the run; the backfill should be invisible on production dashboards. Reconcile per chunk."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "29"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [27]{1}[14]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Airflow/Dagster with pools or concurrency limits; reads from a Postgres read replica. (b) Lakeflow Jobs with limited concurrency; read from a Lakebase child branch or read replica, not the production branch's primary compute."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.10 Dependency-aware orchestrated backfill"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** A backfill spans many slices and many dependent tables; it must run in the right order, at bounded concurrency, and resume after partial failure."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Downstream slices depend on upstream slices. Unbounded parallelism starves production schedules. Some slices are independent and can run in any order; some (SCD2, running totals) cannot."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Let the orchestrator own the backfill as a first-class object:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Airflow 3**: backfills are created through CLI, API or UI and run by the scheduler; a `reprocess_behavior` of `none`, `failed` or `completed` decides whether existing runs are re-run; a backfill-level `max_active_runs` bounds concurrency independently of the DAG's own limit; `--run-backwards` processes newest first; dry-run lists the intervals [30]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "32"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Dagster**: partitioned assets with a `BackfillPolicy`: `single_run()` processes a partition range in one run (efficient for Spark/warehouse engines; code must read `partition_key_range`), while `multi_run(max_partitions_per_run=n)` bounds each run; multi-asset backfills follow the asset graph when partitioning is compatible [31]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**dbt** (≥1.9): `microbatch` incremental strategy with `event_time`, `batch_size`, `begin`, `lookback`; targeted backfills by `--event-time-start/--event-time-end`; `dbt retry` re-runs only failed batches; batches are independent and may run concurrently; times are UTC [22]. `--full-refresh` rebuilds an incremental model from scratch [21]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Order: **reference data and dimensions before facts**; within a table, **oldest-first** when a slice depends on the previous slice's state, **newest-first** when slices are independent and recent data is most valuable (users get useful results sooner, and a stopped backfill leaves the oldest, least-used data missing rather than the newest)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** The orchestrator's unit (a run per logical date) must match the data's unit (a slice); otherwise the backfill is not idempotent. Backfill concurrency must be budgeted against production schedules."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Dry-run lists exactly the intended slices. After the run, the orchestrator's state (all slices succeeded) agrees with the data's state (reconciliation per slice)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "31"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [21]{1}[30]{2}[32]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Airflow 3 backfill / Dagster partitions / dbt microbatch. (b) Lakeflow Jobs **backfill runs** trigger one job run per interval over a range, optionally in parallel, overriding job parameters; the job must use those parameters to select its slice [33]; dbt tasks in Lakeflow Jobs for microbatch."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.11 Late data and restatement"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Records keep arriving for periods already reported (S3), or a correction changes a published number."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Consumers want stable numbers. Completeness is a heuristic (3.1). Regulators or boards may need to know what was reported when."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** (1) Reprocess a **lookback** window on every run (dbt `lookback`, Airflow interval overlap), so lateness within the window is absorbed automatically [22]. (2) Beyond the window, late data triggers an explicit **restatement**: a backfill of the affected slices through the normal path (4.1/4.10), recorded as an event. (3) Keep **transaction time** on published outputs (table snapshots at minimum; row-level `recorded_at` or bitemporal columns where consumers need to see corrections) [10]{1}. (4) Publish a restatement log: which slices, why, old and new totals, logic version."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Numbers change after publication, visibly and with a reason. Lookback costs compute every run."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** For each restated slice, the restatement log shows old and new control totals and links to the run id; time travel to the pre-restatement snapshot reproduces the old number."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "10"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [1]{1}[11]{2}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) dbt microbatch `lookback`; Iceberg tags to pin \"as-reported\" snapshots. (b) Delta time travel and table history; dbt microbatch; a restatement log table."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.12 Logic version stamping"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** After a backfill, outputs produced by different code coexist; nobody can tell which rows came from which logic."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Partial backfills are normal. Debugging and audit require attribution."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Stamp every output row (or at least every slice) with the **logic version** (pipeline version string or code commit) and the **run id**. Treat a change that alters outputs as a version bump (semantic versioning or equivalent), and make the backfill scope \"all slices whose stamped version is older than the target version\". Store run metadata (inputs' versions/snapshot ids, logic version, parameters) in a run table."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** A cheap query answers \"is the backfill complete?\": `SELECT count(*) WHERE logic_version <> :target`. Restated results become attributable. Costs one or two columns."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Invariant check after every backfill: no slice in the target range carries a stale version."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** First principles; aligns with lineage practice (OpenLineage run facets) **[not cited to a specific spec section]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) A column set by the job from its build version; dbt `invocation_id` and a project var. (b) Same; Unity Catalog lineage gives table/column lineage but not per-row logic version, so the column is still needed."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "4.13 Schema evolution during a backfill"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** The schema of source or target changes while a long backfill is running, or history contains old schema versions."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Old records lack new fields; renamed fields break positional loaders; type widening is safe, narrowing is not."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Freeze the target schema for the duration of a backfill, or apply only additive changes (new nullable columns, type widening) that both old and new code tolerate (expand/contract, 5.1). Map each historical source schema version explicitly to the target schema with a versioned mapping; do not rely on positional or \"by name, best effort\" merges. Configure incremental tools to fail on unexpected schema change rather than silently drop columns (dbt `on_schema_change: fail`) **[unverified]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** A schema change mid-backfill becomes a planned step, not a surprise. Table formats that support column ids (Iceberg, Delta column mapping) make renames safe."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Schema snapshot taken at backfill start equals the one at the end, or differs only by recorded additive changes."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [34]; first principles."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Iceberg schema evolution by column id; dbt `on_schema_change`. (b) Delta schema evolution (`mergeSchema`), column mapping mode for renames."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "5. Pattern catalogue: operational databases"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "5.1 Expand, backfill in batches, contract"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Add or change a column on a large, busy table and fill it for existing rows without downtime."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Most `ALTER TABLE` forms take an `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` lock [35]. A single `UPDATE` over millions of rows is one long transaction: it holds row locks, creates a dead tuple per row, and holds back vacuum. Application versions old and new run side by side during deploys."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Parallel change (expand / migrate / contract) [36]:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Expand.** Add the new column as nullable, or with a *non-volatile* default, which in modern Postgres does not rewrite the table [35]. Set a short `lock_timeout` on the DDL so a blocked `ALTER` fails fast instead of queueing all traffic behind it. Deploy code that writes both old and new forms."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Backfill in batches.** Update by primary-key ranges of a few thousand rows, each in its own short transaction, sleeping between batches and throttling on replication lag (4.9). Make each batch idempotent (`WHERE new_col IS NULL`). Record progress."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Constrain without long locks.** Add constraints `NOT VALID`, then `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT`, which takes only `SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE` [35]. Build indexes `CONCURRENTLY`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Migrate reads**, then **contract**: stop writing the old form, drop it once nothing reads it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "Tools automate this. **pgroll** serves old and new schema versions at once through views, backfills new columns in batches, keeps old and new columns in sync with triggers during the migration, and supports start / complete / rollback [37]. **gh-ost** (MySQL) copies into a ghost table while tailing the binlog, throttles, and supports `--test-on-replica` [29]. Stripe's four-step dual-write migration is the application-level version [27]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Several deploys instead of one. A window in which both forms must be kept consistent. Triggers (pgroll) add write overhead."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** `SELECT count(*) WHERE new_col IS NULL` reaches zero; spot-check equivalence of old and new forms; monitor lock waits and replication lag during the run."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "36"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [27]{1}[35]{2}[37]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Postgres + pgroll, or hand-written batches from Airflow / Dagster / a migration tool. (b) Lakebase is Postgres: the same applies; run migrations through CI against a branch first (5.2)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "5.2 Rehearse on a copy-on-write branch"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** An operational backfill or migration must be tested against production-shaped data, at production size, before it touches production."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Forces.** Full copies of big databases are slow and expensive; stale copies hide the very edge cases that break migrations; testing on production is risky."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "39"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Create a **copy-on-write branch** of the production database (at the current point or a past point in time), run the migration and backfill there, measure duration, lock waits and output, then run the same script against production. Neon and Databricks Lakebase branches share storage pages with the parent and only store changes; creation time does not depend on database size and does not load the parent [38]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Rehearsal becomes routine. Branch compute differs from production compute, so timings are indicative, not exact. Branches contain production data, so access to them needs the same controls as production."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Rehearsal produces the same row counts and checks that the production run will be held to; production run is aborted if it diverges materially from the rehearsal."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [38]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Neon branches; for vanilla Postgres, restore a snapshot or use a ZFS/LVM clone. (b) Lakebase branches; the same pattern for Delta is a shallow clone (4.7)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "5.3 Logical replication for initial sync"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Seed a new Postgres (or move to a new cluster) with an existing database's data, then keep it in sync until cutover."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Create a publication on the source and a subscription on the target. By default the subscription **copies existing table data** and then streams changes; temporary table-synchronization slots are created for the initial copy and dropped when it completes [40]. Cut over when lag is near zero: stop writes on the source, wait for lag to reach zero, switch writers, keep the old side as fallback."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Schema (DDL) is not replicated; apply it on both sides. Sequences need resetting at cutover. Initial copy of large tables loads the source; replication slots hold WAL if the subscriber falls behind and can fill the source's disk."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Per-table row counts and hash reconciliation at a quiesced point before cutover (4.8)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [40]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Maps to.** (a) Postgres native logical replication. (b) Lakebase supports Postgres logical replication for inbound sync **[not verified for the current Lakebase release]**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "6. Pattern catalogue: legacy migration"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "6.1 Strangler fig"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Replace a legacy system that holds years of history without a risky big-bang cutover."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "28"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Build the new system beside the old one and move behaviour and data across in increments, keeping transitional architecture (adapters, event interception) that lets both coexist; retire the old parts as they stop being used [41]{1}. For data: migrate one domain, one region or one cohort at a time; each increment uses 6.2–6.4."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Temporary code that will be deleted; a longer period with two systems. Each increment is small enough to roll back."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [41]{1}."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "6.2 Initial load plus CDC cutover (or frozen source)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Move history and keep up with ongoing changes until cutover."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** Two variants:"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Live source.** Snapshot-and-log stitching (4.4) or logical replication (5.3): bulk-load history, stream changes, reconcile, cut over when lag is near zero."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Frozen source.** When the legacy system has no usable change log, agree a **freeze point**: the legacy system becomes read-only (or a point-in-time extract is taken), history up to the freeze is loaded, reconciled and signed off, then writers switch to the new system. Any write after the freeze goes only to the new system."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Frozen-source cutovers need a write outage or a business process pause, but are simplest to verify: there is one fixed source state to reconcile against. Live cutovers avoid the outage but must reconcile a moving target at a known log position."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** Reconciliation (4.8) at the freeze point or at a recorded log position; sign-off recorded with the report."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [14]{1}[40]; first principles."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "6.3 Parallel run and fallback window"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Building confidence that the new system produces the same (or intentionally different) results as the legacy one."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.** For a defined period, run both systems on the same inputs and compare outputs (4.8). Fowler/Thoughtworks describe parallel running as the way to let the business confirm key numbers match before relying on the new system [28]. Keep the legacy system available, read-only or shadow-updated, for a **fallback window** after cutover, with a tested procedure to switch back. Define in advance the exit criteria (e.g. N consecutive cycles with zero unexplained diffs) and the end date of the fallback window."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** Running two systems costs effort; without an end date, parallel runs never end."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** [26]{1}[28]."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "6.4 Quarantine, provenance and PII minimisation"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Problem.** Legacy data is messy, and the backfill must not silently drop bad rows, lose where data came from, or copy more personal data than needed."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Solution.**"
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Quarantine, do not drop.** Rows that fail validation go to a quarantine table with the raw record, the failing rule, and the run id. Counts reconcile as `loaded + quarantined = extracted`. Triage quarantined rows: fix the mapping, fix at source, or accept with a recorded reason."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Provenance per row.** Carry `source_system`, `source_key`, `source_extract_id` (or file hash) and `loaded_by_run` on every loaded row, so any value can be traced to its origin, and loaded history is distinguishable from natively created data."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**PII minimisation.** Decide per column whether it is needed in the new system. Drop, pseudonymise (keyed hash with a managed secret) or generalise before the data lands, not after; data in a landing zone or quarantine table is still data held. Apply the same retention rules to quarantine and backup copies."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Consequences.** More tables to manage; but every row is accounted for and every value is traceable."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Verification.** `extracted = loaded + quarantined` per slice; no row in target lacks provenance columns; a column inventory shows each PII field's treatment."
msgstr ""

#: docs/guides/backfill.md
msgid "**Sources.** First principles; quarantine is the standard dead-letter / \"expectations\" approach (e.g. Lakeflow declarative pipeline expectations, Deequ) [24]."
msgstr ""

